he equation \n\n$$\nv=u\n$$\n\ndefines a straight line, and the coordinates of, say, $(1,-1)$ satisfy the inequality. \n\nTherefore, the region where the transformation is (locally) orientation-reversing must be the following:\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14043620/assets/8382e54c1f91ff0e07910c1250c046413814748d9e7ef8968984b7223b43afcc.png)" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :question/answer-fields ["q-161838/selection"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:knowledge-point/id #uuid "091b8235-56d8-5a08-bc95-08c99772cfd8"] :knowledge-point/questions ["q-161838"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id "q-161837/selection/d16c5ecb86668de04caadc5c537d1f76e51f90f90f855e7835d6ada1006dfea5" :answer/id #uuid "a1323d4b-5d6c-5d05-b7ce-5f224d94e07d" :answer/type :answer.type/image :answer/value "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14043620/assets/3033f0ae73e3f484fc5d3d65e388062feba1824fb3da9971d560cf2f518f49bb.png" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-161837/selection/fe83fda3cf7275396c6ff3701a6aa3c23169c137cdfb604ceed9e4c6bea66ce6" :answer/id #uuid "d088b455-8f2a-557d-9731-a266c019af1b" :answer/type :answer.type/image :answer/value "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14043620/assets/b0171dfd42295e39cd45186e8edba3a5105fbd1bd02a1621223091de75815f61.png" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-161837/selection/aaa809f81513eead9e8b14a16d3008ee2501608e1dfdaf62320a3894c31915f7" :answer/id #uuid "0325c7c6-0b05-54d6-b88a-a3dea017a901" :answer/type :answer.type/image :answer/value "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14043620/assets/cb5b22ff46569b231d122c2ed11bd412907f36271a033d86769435adf9fe48c7.png" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-161837/selection/cca7d83c34d3d3bbf967dbbbf4ef0b969e80de877643f08aaaef80d3e6bdfee8" :answer/id #uuid "284819b2-e283-5689-a01d-e1d9883573b8" :answer/type :answer.type/image :answer/value "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14043620/assets/d477a9f312fc2fb0a5a4c2d13164c96118aa70b53cf23db80be9ec698151e6fd.png" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-161837/selection/601b438e7154588a4d87dc9ef0df74327030a435c33ffb2bc5681f28f65b50cc" :answer/id #uuid "6357bf8b-1482-5211-9a7c-29b32ad00d57" :answer/type :answer.type/image :answer/value "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14043620/assets/d5889bd5e8d537757de25a7b1a868ff8a903da14c9088c5f8de5a70d8b078552.png" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-161837/selection" :answer-field/id #uuid "57de394d-fefc-5046-84e6-3484649715e6" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "q-161837/selection/aaa809f81513eead9e8b14a16d3008ee2501608e1dfdaf62320a3894c31915f7" :answer-field/choices ["q-161837/selection/d16c5ecb86668de04caadc5c537d1f76e51f90f90f855e7835d6ada1006dfea5" "q-161837/selection/fe83fda3cf7275396c6ff3701a6aa3c23169c137cdfb604ceed9e4c6bea66ce6" "q-161837/selection/aaa809f81513eead9e8b14a16d3008ee2501608e1dfdaf62320a3894c31915f7" "q-161837/selection/cca7d83c34d3d3bbf967dbbbf4ef0b969e80de877643f08aaaef80d3e6bdfee8" "q-161837/selection/601b438e7154588a4d87dc9ef0df74327030a435c33ffb2bc5681f28f65b50cc"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "q-161837" :question/id #uuid "c2f0da55-1926-59a4-96c8-e68855af7a42" :question/math-academy-id "q-161837" :question/problem "Which of the following shows the region in the $uv$-plane where the transformation $T,$ given by \n\n$$\nT:[\\begin{aligned}u \\\\ v\\end{aligned}]⟶[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{3}{u}^{3}+v \\\\ uv\\end{aligned}],\n$$\n\nis (locally) orientation-reversing?" :question/worked-solution "Recall that a transformation is (locally) orientation-reversing at the points where the Jacobian determinant is negative:\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}<0\n$$\n\nWe have\n\n$$\nT:[\\begin{aligned}u \\\\ v\\end{aligned}]⟶[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{3}{u}^{3}+v \\\\ uv\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nComputing the corresponding Jacobian determinant, we obtain \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)} & =|\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂x}{∂u} & \\frac{∂x}{∂v} \\\\ \\frac{∂y}{∂u} & \\frac{∂y}{∂v}\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =|\\begin{aligned}{u}^{2} & 1 \\\\ v & u\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & ={u}^{2}\\cdot u-v\\cdot 1 \\\\  & ={u}^{3}-v.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo find the required regions, we solve the following inequality:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{u}^{3}-v & <0 \\\\ v & >{u}^{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nNotice that the equation \n\n$$\nv={u}^{3}\n$$\n\ndefines a cubic function, and the coordinates of, say, $(-1,1)$ satisfy the inequality. \n\nTherefore, the region where the transformation is (locally) orientation-reversing must be the following:\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14043620/assets/cb5b22ff46569b231d122c2ed11bd412907f36271a033d86769435adf9fe48c7.png)" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :question/answer-fields ["q-161837/selection"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:knowledge-point/id #uuid "091b8235-56d8-5a08-bc95-08c99772cfd8"] :knowledge-point/questions ["q-161837"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id "q-161855/selection/a22fd47dba4dd4f2b3c33ffa374e8a90b5282cbff73c91e111676d143a95e9f2" :answer/id #uuid "74763255-fd2c-5df7-8856-04ad1f4cb2ca" :answer/type :answer.type/image :answer/value "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14043620/assets/7fc8ce1e1eaa3eb4e4acbbc167bdc4a479591bea21d2acdeaf799bd99d6b1150.png" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-161855/selection/24650ec12a3914d9e542cd33c1a2dd0f4cbd470ce028dad4e97ea6970dc95eac" :answer/id #uuid "f1122d3f-ff29-5cb8-8015-e8edef091955" :answer/type :answer.type/image :answer/value "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14043620/assets/1368ee41963c1abcc320d2cd35449c08599582f37c7d68f750e137fcdcd2e57b.png" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-161855/selection/61d45611a261b4e887b1c756769da6eb00e09913c496e0cce14bf309047edfb7" :answer/id #uuid "3e4e0673-9394-5e85-b2b7-6fc5f5374903" :answer/type :answer.type/image :answer/value "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14043620/assets/613e83ce5ad6154905ec9001ece078309e396561e251e85455ff261bb4cd61a0.png" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-161855/selection/dbb77d6f213b3196fc60fb2dd773bf651e9cd05126cd55a7382f8fe95923f9d1" :answer/id #uuid "ad5b70e4-6ba4-5d95-bc10-903be175a9b5" :answer/type :answer.type/image :answer/value "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14043620/assets/fe7bb422a3f992ea88a900e63b2c55df99bce3a785929901c248abadfadf23f1.png" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-161855/selection/e85736e8659722e2536b528b95f2f0102696b5e0224cfb669fa168f34456546e" :answer/id #uuid "0503e98c-14fd-5283-aaf2-e95c7136af13" :answer/type :answer.type/image :answer/value "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14043620/assets/9ecab1b0f048e83b57d04a60c1cf20ee5ed0e61257577cf42c1a091976c04473.png" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-161855/selection" :answer-field/id #uuid "b6de591a-b4f0-5e48-bec9-2bb805c1fc06" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "q-161855/selection/61d45611a261b4e887b1c756769da6eb00e09913c496e0cce14bf309047edfb7" :answer-field/choices ["q-161855/selection/a22fd47dba4dd4f2b3c33ffa374e8a90b5282cbff73c91e111676d143a95e9f2" "q-161855/selection/24650ec12a3914d9e542cd33c1a2dd0f4cbd470ce028dad4e97ea6970dc95eac" "q-161855/selection/61d45611a261b4e887b1c756769da6eb00e09913c496e0cce14bf309047edfb7" "q-161855/selection/dbb77d6f213b3196fc60fb2dd773bf651e9cd05126cd55a7382f8fe95923f9d1" "q-161855/selection/e85736e8659722e2536b528b95f2f0102696b5e0224cfb669fa168f34456546e"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "q-161855" :question/id #uuid "79f8fa2f-9fe2-5bd3-a5f8-69303258e3fe" :question/math-academy-id "q-161855" :question/problem "Which of the following shows the region in the $uv$-plane where the transformation $T,$ given by \n\n$$\nT:[\\begin{aligned}u \\\\ v\\end{aligned}]⟶[\\begin{aligned}{e}^{uv} \\\\ {u}^{2}+v\\end{aligned}],\n$$\n\nis (locally) orientation-preserving?" :question/worked-solution "Recall that a transformation is (locally) orientation-preserving at the points where the Jacobian determinant is positive:\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}>0.\n$$\n\nWe have\n\n$$\nT:[\\begin{aligned}u \\\\ v\\end{aligned}]⟶[\\begin{aligned}{e}^{uv} \\\\ {u}^{2}+v\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nComputing the corresponding Jacobian determinant, we obtain \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)} & =|\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂x}{∂u} & \\frac{∂x}{∂v} \\\\ \\frac{∂y}{∂u} & \\frac{∂y}{∂v}\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =|\\begin{aligned}v{e}^{uv} & u{e}^{uv} \\\\ 2u & 1\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =v{e}^{uv}\\cdot (1)-(2u)\\cdot u{e}^{uv} \\\\  & ={e}^{uv}(v-2{u}^{2}).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo find the required regions, we solve the following inequality:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{e}^{uv}(v-2{u}^{2}) & >0 \\\\ v-2{u}^{2} & >0 \\\\ v & >2{u}^{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nNotice that the equation \n\n$$\nv=2{u}^{2}\n$$\n\ndefines a parabola, and the coordinates of, say, $(0,1)$ satisfy the inequality. \n\nTherefore, the region where the transformation is (locally) orientation-preserving must be the following:\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14043620/assets/613e83ce5ad6154905ec9001ece078309e396561e251e85455ff261bb4cd61a0.png)" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :question/answer-fields ["q-161855/selection"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:knowledge-point/id #uuid "091b8235-56d8-5a08-bc95-08c99772cfd8"] :knowledge-point/questions ["q-161855"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id [:question/math-academy-id "e-6810"] :question/problem "Find the Jacobian determinant of the transformation $\\mathbf{T},$ defined as\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14043620/assets/ce4692599c5c9fe69ed9247d65dee5d982e1b7a2cf33b9070b799c94c321bad4.png)" :question/worked-solution "Given a transformation \n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14043620/assets/59265be9c026880542032006b0c4aec9f1c8eeb2cd448423d38c17f75e1393cd.png)\n\nthe Jacobian determinant of $\\mathbf T,$ denoted $\\dfrac{\\partial (x, y)}{\\partial (u, v)} ,$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)} & =|\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂x}{∂u} & \\frac{∂x}{∂v} \\\\ \\frac{∂y}{∂u} & \\frac{∂y}{∂v}\\end{aligned}|.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHere, we have $x=(u+v)^3$ and $y=(u-v)^2.$\n\nFirst, we find the partial derivatives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂x}{∂u} & =3(u+v)^{2} & \\,\\frac{∂x}{∂v} & =3(u+v)^{2} \\\\ \\frac{∂y}{∂u} & =2(u-v) & \\,\\frac{∂y}{∂v} & =-2(u-v)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the corresponding Jacobian determinant is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)} & =|\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂x}{∂u} & \\frac{∂x}{∂v} \\\\ \\frac{∂y}{∂u} & \\frac{∂y}{∂v}\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =|\\begin{aligned}3(u+v)^{2} & 3(u+v)^{2} \\\\ 2(u-v) & -2(u-v)\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =3(u+v)^{2}\\cdot (-2(u-v))-3(u+v)^{2}\\cdot 2(u-v) \\\\  & =-12(u+v)^{2}(u-v).\\end{aligned}\n$$" :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:question/math-academy-id "e-11156"] :question/problem "For the transformation $\\mathbf{T},$ defined as\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14043620/assets/eec66e1a6fdb16b157ec4ba8ea32b8e2328c26910594a7d21bb7346669797627.png)\n\ncompute the value of the Jacobian determinant at the point where ![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14043620/assets/01df906c78b9ed5b4d6f1e29b4aeeb3be607b7570db357c4dce5949702432011.png)" :question/worked-solution "Given a transformation \n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14043620/assets/62dec67282c5bc2707377cb976412a48ca3c41ffeed6aa74ef0d5b1ef8d6e380.png)\n\nthe Jacobian determinant of $\\mathbf T,$ denoted $\\dfrac{\\partial (x, y)}{\\partial (u, v)} ,$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)} & =|\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂x}{∂u} & \\frac{∂x}{∂v} \\\\ \\frac{∂y}{∂u} & \\frac{∂y}{∂v}\\end{aligned}|.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHere, we have $x(u,v) = 2v\\cos{u}$ and $y(u,v) = v\\sin{u}.$\n\nFirst, we find the partial derivatives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂x}{∂u} & =-2v\\operatorname{sin}⁡u & \\,\\frac{∂x}{∂v} & =2\\operatorname{cos}⁡u \\\\ \\frac{∂y}{∂u} & =v\\operatorname{cos}⁡u & \\,\\frac{∂y}{∂v} & =\\operatorname{sin}⁡u\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the corresponding Jacobian determinant is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)} & =|\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂x}{∂u} & \\frac{∂x}{∂v} \\\\ \\frac{∂y}{∂u} & \\frac{∂y}{∂v}\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =|\\begin{aligned}-2v\\operatorname{sin}⁡u & 2\\operatorname{cos}⁡u \\\\ v\\operatorname{cos}⁡u & \\operatorname{sin}⁡u\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =-2v\\operatorname{sin}⁡u\\cdot \\operatorname{sin}⁡u-2\\operatorname{cos}⁡u\\cdot v\\operatorname{cos}⁡u \\\\  & =-2v({sin}^{2}⁡u+{cos}^{2}⁡u) \\\\  & =-2v.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, when ![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14043620/assets/72902f1a278bc1316ff58c54940b4d5e65fa04aa8e1711c8e0a1726f57cd3641.png) the value of our Jacobian determinant is\n\n$$\n\\left.\\dfrac{\\partial (x, y)}{\\partial (u, v)}\\right|_{(0,-1)} = -2(-1) = 2.\n$$" :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:question/math-academy-id "e-12417"] :question/problem "Consider a small rectangle $\\Delta$ whose bottom-left corner is located at the point $(-1,2)$ with sides parallel to the coordinate axes in the $uv$-plane and whose area is $\\delta{A}.$ Find an approximation for the area of the image of this rectangle under the action of the transformation $\\mathbf T,$ given by\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14043620/assets/4c41597b6c647ba16bff7b6675d1bbe9d3c66b8cbb8e9a13bbb6651da6cd284e.png)" :question/worked-solution "Recall that the absolute value of the Jacobian determinant gives the local area scale factor of the transformation at that point:\n\n$$\n\\textrm{Area}\\big(\\mathbf{T}(\\Delta)\\big) \\approx \\left| \\dfrac{\\partial (x, y)}{\\partial (u, v)} \\right| \\cdot \\underbrace{\\textrm{Area}(\\Delta)}_{\\large \\delta{A}}\n$$\n\n \n\nComputing the corresponding Jacobian determinant, we obtain the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)} & =|\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂x}{∂u} & \\frac{∂x}{∂v} \\\\ \\frac{∂y}{∂u} & \\frac{∂y}{∂v}\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =|\\begin{aligned}{v}^{3} & 3u{v}^{2} \\\\ 2u & 2v\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & ={v}^{3}\\cdot 2v-3u{v}^{2}\\cdot 2u \\\\  & =2{v}^{4}-6{u}^{2}{v}^{2} \\\\ {\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}|}_{(-1,2)} & =(2{v}^{4}-6{u}^{2}{v}^{2})\\,{|}_{u=-1\\,v=2} \\\\  & =2(2)^{4}-6(-1)^{2}(2)^{2} \\\\  & =32-24 \\\\  & =8\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{Area}(T(Δ)) & ≈|8|\\cdot \\delta A \\\\  & =8\\,\\delta A.\\end{aligned}\n$$" :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:question/math-academy-id "e-12418"] :question/problem "Sketch the region in the $uv$-plane where the transformation $\\mathbf{T},$ given by \n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14043620/assets/0e620d5ca71a48c87a0d5942ffa8378964f6172bff7396c290024a5299531204.png)\nis (locally) orientation-reversing." :question/worked-solution "Recall that a transformation is (locally) orientation-reversing at the points where the Jacobian determinant is negative:\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\partial (x, y)}{\\partial (u, v)}  <    0\n$$\n\nWe have\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14043620/assets/e947cb962ec1c0405292be18c838205b7be95ab083e07925d797b87e9c5b8654.png)\n\nComputing the corresponding Jacobian determinant, we obtain \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)} & =|\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂x}{∂u} & \\frac{∂x}{∂v} \\\\ \\frac{∂y}{∂u} & \\frac{∂y}{∂v}\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =|\\begin{aligned}{e}^{v} & u{e}^{v} \\\\ 2u & -2v\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & ={e}^{v}\\cdot (-2v)-u{e}^{v}\\cdot 2u \\\\  & =-2{e}^{v}({u}^{2}+v).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo find the required regions, we solve the following inequality:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-2{e}^{v}({u}^{2}+v) & <0 \\\\ {u}^{2}+v & >0 \\\\ v & >-{u}^{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nNotice that the equation \n\n$$\nv=-u^2\n$$\n\ndefines a downward-opening parabola, and the coordinates of, say, $(1,1)$ satisfy the inequality. \n\nTherefore, the region where the transformation is (locally) orientation-reversing must be the following:\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14043620/assets/2b6128c5a1737e3ad1fd82f91f5ccd320421ad3472ae8152440034ecea10d364.png)" :db/ensure [:question/validate]}]
